.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:1:# Pareto and selection
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:3:The current multi-ECC study uses Pareto analysis for audit and a deterministic lexicographic winner rule for selection. They answer different questions: Pareto membership asks whether any feasible candidate is no worse in every objective; the winner rule chooses one minimum-energy feasible record.
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:28:    E -->|yes| F["Compute Pareto set"]
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:31:    G -->|yes| J["Lexicographic minimum energy, complexity, encoded bits, ID"]
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:40:3. modelled total energy in J/scenario;
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:44:Every direction is `min`; epsilon is `0.0`. The winner rule then minimizes `(modelled_total_energy, decoder_complexity_proxy, encoded_bits, implementation_id)` among feasible candidates. It is not a weighted score and does not select the plotted knee.
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:72:## Hypervolume
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:74:The 2D documentation audit computes exact dominated area against a data-derived reference point 5% worse than the maximum plotted Pareto objective in each dimension. Units are the product of the plotted units and the reference is stored in figure data. Duplicates are de-duplicated geometrically. Hypervolume is not used to choose a winner.
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:78:`green_ecc_phy/pareto_validation.py` does not import the study selector's private `_pareto` or `_dominates`. `scripts/generate_documentation_figures.py` independently recomputes all 192 five-objective frontiers after constraints and requires exact identifier agreement with `scenario_selection_results.json`; any mismatch stops figure generation.
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:80:Targeted tests cover dominated/equal points, mixed directions, nulls, rejected/infeasible candidates, one/zero-candidate fronts, epsilon boundaries, crowding, knee and hypervolume:
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:83:python -m pytest -q tests/python/test_documentation_pareto.py
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:86:![Analytical reliability-cost Pareto frontier](figures/reliability_cost_pareto_analytical.svg)
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:88:*Orange marks the independently reproduced five-objective frontier; grey mutes dominated candidates, crosses are infeasible, and rings distinguish winner and projected knee. Data includes objectives, epsilon, constraint order, hypervolume reference and scenario hash: [`figure_data/reliability_cost_pareto_analytical.json`](figure_data/reliability_cost_pareto_analytical.json).*
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:90:![Analytical energy versus structural complexity](figures/energy_complexity_pareto_analytical.svg)
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:92:*Logic depth is a structural proxy—not measured latency. Data: [`figure_data/energy_complexity_pareto_analytical.json`](figure_data/energy_complexity_pareto_analytical.json).*
.\docs\PARETO_AND_SELECTION.md:104:A physical Pareto frontier is **not computable** because all required physical objectives are null and every generated characterization is structural-only or unavailable. `select-physical` correctly returns `winner: null`. No empty axes or proxy-to-physical substitution is presented as a frontier.
